WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.099
Larry Krasner wants to fix America's

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.929
criminal justice system, which

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:05.819
imprisons more people per capita

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.229
than any other country on the

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:08.489
planet.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.319
Since 2018, he served

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.489
as the district attorney of

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.379
Philadelphia, one of America's

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.359
most highly incarcerated

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.459
and as of late, increasingly

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.589
crime ridden cities.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.629
Krasner spent three decades

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.219
as a criminal and civil rights

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.869
defense attorney before deciding to

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:26.489
run for office.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:27.959
Our movement did the uncomfortable

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.059
thing. We took back power,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.799
he wrote in a memoir about his

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.869
successful 2017 run

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.189
for Philadelphia's district

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.449
attorney.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.369
We outsiders went inside

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.289
and took over the institution we

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.409
had fought against all our lives.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.419
In his first week as D.A.,

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.639
Krasner fired 31 staffers

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.439
and replaced them with a new team

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.599
that he described as ideologically

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:52.949
attached to the mission.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.359
It's a pretty basic mission for

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:55.889
people who are in favor of freedom.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.779
One of those missions is to be

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:59.249
less incarcerated than Vladimir

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.719
Putin's Russia.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:01.799
I don't think that should be very

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.839
controversial. Apparently it is.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.339
But it seems to me that that's

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.649
basic. Another aspect of the mission

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.049
is not to have

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:11.429
what I would call the ultimate form

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.319
of big government, which is to be

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:14.759
the most incarcerated country in the

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.739
world without a perceptible

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.539
increase in safety.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.549
Krasner easily won reelection

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.589
last year, but today he's

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.629
under intense pressure

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.839
because Philly posted a record 562

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.789
murders in 2021,

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.499
and it's on pace for a similar

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.719
outcome in 2022.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.789
The Republican led state legislature

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:37.739
has begun impeachment proceedings

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:38.779
against him.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.609
Reason sat down with Krassner in

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:42.109
his office to talk about his

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:43.099
reforms.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.929
The violent crime spike, the

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:46.669
heat that progressive prosecutors

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:48.259
are feeling in cities like Los

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.179
Angeles and San Francisco,

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.489
and what that means for the future

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:54.439
of the American criminal justice

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:55.440
reform movement.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.209
You spent decades

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:04.489
as a basic civil rights defense

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.009
attorney, and

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:08.779
now you are the city's top

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:09.679
prosecutor.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.809
What made you decide to

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:13.759
pursue that in 2017?

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:14.929
You know, when I came out of law

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:15.769
school, I think I was a little bit

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:17.719
naive. And I believed

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:18.919
that if you just did really good

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.329
work, either as a prosecutor or

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:21.499
defense attorney.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.939
I actually started out as a state

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.679
public defender in that

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.629
under-resourced rodeo

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:28.399
that Justice Sotomayor talked so

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.359
much about.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:30.559
I believe that if you were on either

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:31.909
side, you could do really good work

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:33.799
and that the system wasn't

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.689
broken. But what I found out over 30

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:37.069
years is that in many ways it really

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.139
is. And those were the same 30

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.149
years when much

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:42.589
of the rise of mass incarceration

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:43.590
occurred.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.979
So I got to the point when I was 56

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:47.629
where I felt like in order to have a

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:49.459
much more sweeping impact on

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:50.359
a system that I thought was

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.339
profoundly broken, I

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.209
had to do something else.

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:54.739
And that's when I decided to run to

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.959
be a chief prosecutor in Philly.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.489
And can you talk a little bit about

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.289
Philadelphia specifically?

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.629
Because I know that, for instance,

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:04.489
the youth incarceration

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:05.689
rate was

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.259
high compared to even other big

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:10.669
American cities.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:12.829
I mean, what was the situation that

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:14.899
you were coming into in

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.239
this city that you felt was needing

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:18.559
to be urgently addressed?

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.539
So crazy irony of Philly is

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:22.579
that this is

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.009
a tourist destination for freedom.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:26.539
Liberty Bell, you know the place

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:27.709
where they wrote the Declaration of

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.719
Independence, the Constitution, all

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.009
of that is here. And yet when we

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.139
came into office, it was

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:34.999
the most incarcerated of the big

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.739
cities. It was the poorest of the

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:38.389
big cities. It had the highest level

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:40.069
of supervision on probation and

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.439
parole, 25 times higher rate

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:43.969
than New York City.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:45.919
When we came in here and yet it is a

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.019
majority minority city and it's also

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.300
a city that's about 85% Democratic

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.599
and arguably

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.489
the poorest and the most violent

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:56.719
of the ten biggest cities, most

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.039
chronically violent, anyway, at that

00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.869
time. So how the hell did you

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:01.459
end up with this witch's brew?

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:03.289
How is everything so bad all at

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:04.290
the same time?

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:07.519
That's when we came in, and

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:08.869
that was the mission we faced.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.789
And part of the mission was

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:12.379
trying to figure out how you unravel

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:14.209
that, that unique combination of

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:16.219
economic failure that seems

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.139
connected to criminal justice

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:20.179
policies that don't make you safer

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.648
and that tear the city down.

00:04:21.649 --> 00:04:23.599
That was the mission's big mission.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:24.889
We've made, I think, some

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:26.869
significant progress

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:28.399
while simultaneously slamming into

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:29.959
the pandemic, which has been a

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.759
challenge all across the country.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.469
Some reforms that you implemented

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:35.989
coming in that will probably sound

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:37.729
pretty good to a libertarian

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:40.159
audience, such as basically

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.919
not, uh, declining to prosecute

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:44.959
marijuana crimes,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.999
opioid possession,

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.189
sex work type crimes.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:50.659
You know what what libertarians

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:52.369
would call victimless crimes.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.879
What were you hoping to achieve

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.159
with that policy and how

00:04:58.160 --> 00:04:59.660
has it panned out so far from?

